# ENLIGHTENED: THE ACADEMY ERA, BOOK 1, "THE KING AND THE CLIMB"

## Chapter Twenty-Three: The Underdog

*"**Say thank you to the ones who taught you before you're old enough to understand what it cost them.**"*

*Gratitude-month card, Year 1 cohort packet*

Nothing goes up on July second.

The hall stays full past six on a Thursday now and it has done since the notice moved the block, so there's a sitting at the far end still eating when the Monday crowd would be gone.

Corw checks anyway. He's at the corridor end of the hall before the trays are out and he comes back down the aisle with his hands empty and sits without squaring the tray, which is how I know before he opens his mouth.

"Nothing," Corw says.

"You said nothing would go up," Doyle says.

"I said it isn't that kind of day," Corw says.

"That's the same," Doyle says.

"It's not the same," Corw says.

Fenn has her tray at the angle she keeps it at and hasn't moved since I sat down.

"You wanted to be wrong," Fenn says.

"I wanted somebody to have noticed," Corw says.

"Two thousand of us," Fenn says, "and you're the one who noticed, and now you want a second one."

"One," Corw says. "I want one."

Denic comes in with the collar of his kit still open and gets down the bench in three strides and doesn't sit.

"It's up," Denic says.

"Nothing's up," Corw says.

"The bay four one's up," Denic says. "The Tuesday one."

Doyle has already got both stacks out and he goes down the thin one to the middle and takes one out and turns it face up on the wood and doesn't say anything.

"You've had that since when," Denic says.

"Ten past five," Doyle says.

"I was awake at ten past five," Denic says.

"You were awake at ten past five in the dorm," Doyle says. "It went up in the wing."

Denic gets it off the wood and holds it out at the length of his arm the way he holds everything he reads and gets about four seconds into it.

"Read it out," Petra says.

"It's a line," Denic says.

"Read the line," Petra says.

"Voss, D. Cockpit aptitude, band two, provisional," Denic says, and puts it down. "That's it. That's the whole of it."

"Band two out of what?" Petra says.

"It doesn't say," Denic says.

"It's out of five," Ilyen says, without looking up. "One's the top of it."

Denic stops with his hand still on the notice.

"Six hours," Denic says. "Six hours in a chair on a Tuesday and I get a line and a comma."

"You got band two," Doyle says.

"I got a comma," Denic says.

"Band two's the second band," Corw says. "He's one off top. In year one. In July."

"Nobody gets one in year one," Ilyen says.

"I did," Denic says. "Then why does it say provisional?"

"Because they'll do it again," Ilyen says.

Denic sits down at that, finally, and takes about half a breath and then he's got both hands flat on the table on either side of the notice.

"When?" Denic says.

"It doesn't say," Ilyen says.

"Then I'm ready for it now," Denic says. "I'm in the chair Sunday. Every Sunday. I'll do the six hours on my own if there's nobody to run it, I'll sit in the frame and run the set off the board and I'll do it till it's band one."

"There's no band one in year one," Corw says.

"Then band one in year two," Denic says.

Doyle takes the notice back off the wood, squares it against the edge of the table, and puts it on the thick stack instead of the thin one.

"You'll do two Sundays," Doyle says.

"I'll do all of them," Denic says.

"You'll do two," Doyle says, and goes back to the stack.

Petra's got her chin nearly on the wood again and she's been quiet for the whole of it, which with Petra is a position rather than a mood.

"Corw," Petra says.

"What?" Corw says.

"How's the leg," Petra says.

"There's nothing wrong with my leg," Corw says.

"How's the hand, then," Petra says.

Corw puts his fork down.

"It's fine," Corw says.

"Fine how?" Petra says.

"Fine the way a hand is," Corw says.

"Then what did you do?" Petra says.

"I went down at nine and I was out by twenty past," Corw says. "There's a chair and a man and a card. He puts it in the back of your neck and it takes about as long as a strap does and then you sit for ten minutes so somebody can look at you, and then you walk out, and that's it. That's all of it. And I have been waiting six days for something to happen and nothing has happened."

"Something happened," Fenn says.

"Nothing's happened," Corw says.

"A hundred and thirty happened," Fenn says.

"Don't," Corw says.

Denic has come all the way back round from the notice now and he's leaning across Doyle's stacks with his elbow going where the stacks are.

"Throw something at him," Denic says.

"No," Fenn says.

"He wants to know," Denic says. "Throw something at him and we'll all watch."

"You'll throw it and he'll catch it and we'll all say he caught it faster," Ilyen says, "and none of us will know anything."

Denic sits back down.

"She's right," Doyle says.

"I know she's right," Denic says.

Doyle finishes the thin stack, puts it on the thick one, and then reaches down beside the bench and comes up with a steel rule off his own bench, a metre of it, folded once, and stands it on the table on its end.

"Nobody's throwing anything," Doyle says.

"What's that," Denic says.

"It's a rule," Doyle says.

"I know it's a rule," Denic says.

"Then don't ask what it is," Doyle says.

He unfolds it and holds it up by the top with the numbers running down toward the table and puts two fingers of his other hand out at the bottom of it, and looks at Corw.

"Put your finger and your thumb either side," Doyle says. "Don't touch it. I drop it, you catch it, and I read where your thumb is."

Corw looks at it for a second.

"That's it," Corw says.

"That's it," Doyle says.

"That's not measuring anything," Corw says.

"It falls the same every time," Doyle says. "It doesn't care whose hand it is. The only part of it that changes is how far down the rule you are when you shut your hand."

Corw gets his finger and thumb round it without touching, and Doyle drops it, and Corw shuts his hand about a third of the way down.

"One eighty-one," Doyle says.

"Is that good?" Corw says.

"I don't know," Doyle says.

"You've got the number," Corw says.

"I've got one number," Doyle says. "I'll do ten and take the middle of them, and then I'll have a number for you. And a number for you on its own doesn't say anything either, because I never had a number for you in June."

Corw doesn't answer that.

"Say the rest," Fenn says.

"There isn't a rest," Doyle says.

"Say it anyway," Fenn says.

"He bought it on the Saturday and I could have had ten drops off him on the Friday," Doyle says. "Not to me he didn't say it. So whatever I write down for him this morning is a number on its own. It'll still be a number on its own when he's forty."

Corw has his hands flat again.

"It's a hundred and thirty points," Corw says.

"It's not the points," Doyle says.

Doyle does the ten anyway. Corw goes one eighty-one, one seventy-four, one eighty-eight, and then seven more, and Doyle writes each one on the back of the notice he already read, and doesn't say the middle out loud.

Then he does me.

I get my finger and thumb either side of it and I watch the top of the rule and not his hand, which he tells me to stop doing, so I watch his hand.

"One ninety-nine," Doyle says.

We do it ten times. Denic counts out the drops the way Corw counts out everything else and gets to seven and forgets and starts saying eight twice.

"One ninety-seven," Doyle says, at the end. "That's the middle of yours."

"Is that good?" I say.

"It's a number," Doyle says.

He does Denic and Denic goes one seventy-six and Doyle looks at the paper for a second longer than he looks at anybody else's and writes it down and says nothing at all about it. He does Ilyen, who goes two hundred and three and is entirely untroubled by it. He does Petra. He offers it to Fenn and Fenn shakes her head once without taking her hand off her chin.

"You don't want to know," Denic says.

"I don't want to be on his paper," Fenn says.

Reqa comes past the end of the bench with a coupling in one hand on the way to somewhere else, and stops, and reads the paper upside down for about as long as it takes her to read anything.

"The rule's bent," Reqa says.

"It's not bent," Doyle says.

"It's bent about eleven inches up," Reqa says. "It doesn't matter for this. It matters if you ever use it for what it's for."

She goes. Doyle folds the rule and unfolds it and looks at eleven inches up and folds it again.

"It's bent," Doyle says.

Friday is four hours and the four hours are the same four hours they've been since January.

The floor takes a thousand of us and it never looks like a thousand because the squares are spread and the return puts you back where you started rather than where you finished. You come off, you get walked, you get patched, and the doors put you out at the top of the queue on the wall side. Then you find somebody. That's the part nobody teaches you and the part that eats the morning, because finding somebody means standing in the line and waiting for the line to give you a square, or it means going and asking.

I've got a way of doing it. I stand about six from the front where the line bends and I let two go past me and take the third, because the third is generally somebody who's been beaten and wants a different result and will move fast to get it. It works. It's worked since about April.

By the end of the first hour I've had four and won three.

By the end of the second I've had nine and won seven, and my shoulder is where the shims put it and my arm goes where I send it, and the one I lose I lose to a Hiopi girl a year up who takes my legs out from under me with two of hers and doesn't stay to watch the pull.

Mikka Reyes is in front of me in the queue at the start of the third hour, and then she isn't.

I don't see her go. I get to the bend and let two past and take the third and come off the square eleven minutes later with a win on it, and she's coming back through the doors on the wall side at the same time I am, and she's three squares down and into another one before I've got to the bend.

I get to the bend. I let two past. I take the third.

She's in the far corner with somebody from Vrassik's group, one of the big Osnius boys, and she loses that one and comes off it laughing.

I take the third one again and win it.

She's down the wall with a human kid I don't know and I don't see the end of it.

Fourth hour, I'm at the bend and Vrassik comes up the line on the other side of it, four legs and all of him, with his lance already back on his shoulder and hide gone the flat colour it goes when he's been beaten and hasn't decided how he feels about it.

"How many?" Vrassik says.

"Thirteen," I say.

"Fourteen," Vrassik says.

"Then you're ahead," I say.

"I'm not ahead," Vrassik says, and puts his chin at the far corner without moving anything else. "Reyes has been in twice while we've been standing here saying this."

I look where he's looking. She comes off the square, gets walked, goes through the doors, and is out of them and into the wall-side queue while the doors are still shutting behind her, and there's a boy waiting at the front of it who she hasn't met and she has him by the elbow and into a square in about nine seconds.

"She loses a lot," I say.

"She loses more than you and me together," Vrassik says.

"Then she's not ahead," I say.

"She's in the square," Vrassik says. "That's the whole of it. She's in the square and you're here," and he goes up the line and takes the first one he comes to.

I finish on seventeen.

The board goes up in the combat corridor twenty minutes after the doors shut and about sixty of us are standing at it, and Corw is at the front of the sixty because Corw is always at the front of the sixty.

He doesn't say anything for long enough that Denic goes round him to see it himself.

"Reyes," Denic says.

"Twenty-three," Corw says.

"Say that again," Denic says.

"Twenty-three," Corw says. "I've got sixteen. Trav's got seventeen. Vrassik's got nineteen and Vrassik has never been under seventeen since March. Reyes has twenty-three."

"Who's Reyes," Denic says.

"She's in our rune block," Ilyen says, from the back of it, where she can't possibly be seeing the board. "Two rows up, on the door side. She's been in our rune block since January."

"I know her," I say.

"You don't," Corw says.

"I know her face," I say.

"That's not the same," Corw says.

Mikka comes down the corridor about a minute later with her kit over one shoulder and her hair still wet from the wash, and stops at the edge of the sixty rather than pushing into it, and reads the top of the board from where she is, which takes her about two seconds.

"That's me," Mikka says.

"That's you," Corw says.

"Good," Mikka says.

And then she doesn't say anything else, and the sixty are all looking at her and she's looking at the board with her thumb hooked under the strap of her kit and she is, as far as I can see from where I am, entirely finished with it.

"How?" Corw says.

"How what?" Mikka says.

"You're not top ten," Corw says. "You've never been top ten. I've had a list since February and you have never been on the list."

"I don't know what your list is," Mikka says.

"It's who beats who," Corw says.

"Then I wouldn't be on it," Mikka says. "I got beaten seventeen times today."

Corw stops.

"Seventeen," Corw says.

"About seventeen," Mikka says. "I stopped counting in the second hour. Is that on the board?"

"No," Corw says.

"Then it doesn't matter," Mikka says.

She gets her kit up on the shoulder properly, and on the way past she says something to Denic about his collar being open again and Denic looks down at his own collar, and she says four words to the Osnius boy from the far corner that I don't have any of, and he says four back, and she's gone up the corridor before Corw has got anything else out.

"Forty," Denic says.

"Forty what?" Corw says.

"Twenty-three and seventeen," Denic says. "She was in a square forty times. I had eleven. Trav had twenty-two."

"That's not possible in four hours," Corw says.

"It's six minutes a square with nothing left in it anywhere," Denic says. "It's possible. It's just not possible standing in the line."

"She never waited," Ilyen says.

Nobody says anything to that.

"She'll be nowhere next week," Corw says.

"Probably," Ilyen says.

Saturday, once combat lets out, I go down to the medical wing.

It's the second corridor on the far side of the arena wing and it's got a window in it and a man behind the window with a slate and a queue of two, and both of the two ahead of me are there about a knee, still in half their kit from the floor.

The man gets to me and turns the slate over.

"Name," the man says.

"Trav Calder," I say.

"What are we doing," the man says.

"The one at the top of the third board," I say.

"It's got a name," the man says.

"Quickstep," I say.

He turns the slate back and does something to it and then puts a card up on the counter with the print facing me, and it's eleven lines and the lines are close together and one of them is longer than the rest of them.

I read it.

The man doesn't say anything while I do it. There's somebody behind me who moves their feet twice. The long line has a word in it I have to go back to the start of twice before it comes apart, and then it's a word about the neck and it's ordinary, and the rest of it after that goes clean enough.

I get to the bottom of it.

"Yes," I say.

"One hundred and thirty," the man says.

"Yes," I say.

"Say it," the man says.

"One hundred and thirty," I say.

He does something else to the slate and turns it round and there's a line on it with my name and a number after it, and the number is twenty thousand four hundred and ninety.

"That's what's left," the man says.

"All right," I say.

He waits about a second longer than he needs to and then he takes the slate back.

"Chair's through there," the man says.

There's a chair and there's a woman and it takes about as long as a strap does. She puts it into the back of my neck low down where the collar sits and it's cold going in and then it's nothing at all, and she tells me to keep the collar off it for a day, and then I sit for ten minutes on a bench with the two knees while she does something at the other end of the room and comes back and looks at me for about four seconds and tells me to go.

I'm back through the bay door inside half an hour, and Reqa's got the housing open again with her hands in it.

"Rail's fine," Reqa says.

"I know," I say.

"I did it again this morning anyway," Reqa says.

"You said September," I say.

"I said about September," Reqa says.

Monday morning Corw reads the boards before the trays are out, the same as he reads them every morning, and he comes down the aisle and doesn't sit.

"There's a hundred and thirty in his spend column," Corw says. "First one since January."

I get my tray squared.

Corw's talking to the table, not to me.

"You never spend," Corw says. "Not once, all year, and you didn't say a word to any of us before you did it."

"How much was in it before?" Denic says.

"Twenty thousand six hundred and twenty, Friday night," Corw says.

"Then what's wrong with it," Denic says.

"Nothing's wrong with it," Corw says. "That's the part. Your points are impossible and you spent a hundred and thirty of them on a patch and told nobody."

"Say it as a fraction," Petra says.

"I'm not saying it as a fraction," Corw says.

"You've already done it as a fraction," Fenn says.

"One in a hundred and fifty-eight," Corw says.

"There it is," Fenn says.

"It's a good patch," Denic says.

"I know it's a good patch," Corw says.

"Then what do you want?" Denic says.

Corw sits down. He squares the tray. He doesn't touch it.

"Nothing," Corw says.

Doyle has the rule up out of the bag before anybody's finished, folded once, and he stands it on the table on its end and looks at me.

"Ten," Doyle says.

"Now," I say.

"Now, and again Thursday, and again the Thursday after," Doyle says, "because I am not doing this twice in one lifetime."

I put my finger and thumb either side of it.

We do the ten. Denic counts them and gets to eight this time. The rule comes down and my hand shuts and it keeps coming down and my hand shuts and after about the fourth one I stop watching Doyle's hand and just have the rule.

Doyle writes each one on the paper under Thursday's numbers and then he sits with it for a while.

"One eighty-four," Doyle says.

"Out of?" Denic says.

"It's not out of anything," Doyle says. "It's millimetres. He was a hundred and ninety-seven on Thursday and he's a hundred and eighty-four this morning."

"That's thirteen," Corw says.

"Thirteen millimetres?" Denic says.

"Yes," Doyle says.

"That's nothing," Denic says. "That's the width of my thumb."

"It's thirteen every time," Doyle says. "It was thirteen on the first drop and it'll be thirteen on Thursday. It doesn't vary. That's what it is."

Denic looks at his own thumb for a second.

"Can you feel it?" Denic says, at me.

"No," I say.

"Nothing at all?" Denic says.

"No," I say.

"Corw couldn't feel his either," Fenn says.

"I never said I couldn't feel mine," Corw says.

"You said it for six days," Fenn says.

"I said nothing had happened," Corw says. "Something happened. It's on his paper. It happened to me too and I just haven't got the paper for it," and he says the last part fast and then stops.

Petra puts her elbows on the table.

"Say the rest," Petra says.

"There isn't a rest," Corw says.

"There's a rest," Petra says.

Corw looks at the middle of the table.

"I said four hundred and six out loud on the Sunday," Corw says. "At this table. Nobody did the arithmetic. I already had. Four hundred and six is three of them and change. So I sat on it. Six days. Never sat on anything for six days in my life. Because if I go down there and it comes back short, everything I've said since January is a boy talking. So I waited. It was fine. Chair, a man, a card. Twenty minutes."

Nobody says anything for a second.

"You got it," Fenn says.

"I got it," Corw says.

"Then that's all it was," Fenn says.

Doyle's still got the Tuesday notice, the one with Corw's first numbers written on the back of it Thursday, and he turns it over and reads down it without anybody asking him to.

"One seventy-nine," Doyle says.

Corw stops with his fork halfway up.

"One seventy-nine what?" Corw says.

"Middle of ten. Thursday. First one I tested." Doyle taps the page. "You've had it since June. I never said it out loud."

Corw puts the fork down.

"That was already patched," Corw says. "Five days in by Thursday. That's not a before."

"I know," Doyle says. "Wrote it down anyway."

Corw looks at where mine sits on the same page.

"Five under his," Corw says. "Patch for patch."

"You didn't know that an hour ago," Fenn says.

"I know it now," Corw says.

I don't say anything.

Denic has both hands flat and has been building something behind his face for the whole of it.

"Right," Denic starts.

"No," Doyle says.

"You don't know what I'm going to say," Denic says.

"You're going to say all eight of us," Doyle says.

"All eight of us," Denic says. "By the end of August. Reqa and Fenn and Petra and you as well, because it's the same neck whether you fly or not, and I'll work out who's short and we'll cover whoever's short off the Fridays, and Doyle takes everybody's number first this time, before anybody goes anywhere near that window."

"Meks don't get put in a square," Reqa says, from the end of the bench, without her head coming up.

"You'd still be faster," Denic says.

"At what?" Reqa says.

Denic doesn't have one for that.

"Two of you will do it," Doyle says. "Reqa and Petra."

"All eight," Denic says.

"Two," Doyle says, and puts the paper on the thick stack.

Ilyen has finished eating and she has her hands flat either side of the tray the way she has them flat either side of everything, and she's been out of it since Corw said the fraction.

"He won't buy another one," Ilyen says.

"He's got twenty thousand," Denic says.

"I know what he's got," Ilyen says.

"Then he'll buy another one," Denic says.

"He won't," Ilyen says, and picks her tray up, and goes.

Doyle reads the notice at the last meal on Wednesday and it's the twenty-seventh one since January by Corw's count, and it's a list.

"Eleven names," Doyle says.

"Whose?" Corw says.

"I don't know eight of them," Doyle says.

"Read them," Corw says.

Doyle reads them. There are two from our rune block and one is a Hiopi boy from the far bay and the rest are nobody I've heard of, and at the bottom of it there's a date in August and a word I do know because it's been on the wall in the entrance hall since the day I got here, which is Cradle.

"Where are they going?" Denic says.

"It doesn't say," Doyle says.

"What's it for," Denic says.

"It doesn't say that either," Doyle says.

Corw takes it off him and goes down it twice, which for Corw is once more than anything gets.

"It's not a ranking," Corw says.

"No," Doyle says.

"There's nobody from the top fifty on it," Corw says. "There's nobody from the bottom fifty on it either. I know where nine hundred of us sit and these eleven are all over the place."

"So it isn't about that," Doyle says.

"It isn't about anything I can see," Corw says, and hands it back, and gets his tray, and that's the end of it.

Doyle puts it on the thick stack.

Mikka Reyes is nineteenth on the board the Friday after, and eleventh the Friday after that, and by the end of July nobody at our table says her name unless Doyle is reading a number off a page. She sits two rows up on the door side in the rune block and she's there every morning and she has been there every morning since January.

The last Friday of July I finish on eighteen.

The arena wing is at sixteen when I get down there in the evening and the woman at the desk has the slate up in front of her face and doesn't move it, and the lights past the fortieth are off, and I go up the ladder and get four straps in, twelve and stop, twelve and stop.

I get seven.

The seventh is a girl a year up with a longer reach than mine who has put me on my back twice this month, and it goes about ninety seconds and then it doesn't.

I come off with twenty-five minutes still on the lights and my hands doing the ordinary shake they do after the last one, and the corridor down from the arena has both doors wedged and the six boards are under their own light with nobody in front of them.

I go past.

The bay's got the compressor running and Reqa's under the rig with the seat out again and one boot flat on the floor.

"Seven," I say.

"I know," Reqa says.

"How?" I say.

"Fenn was down there," Reqa says.

She comes out from under it and gets the rag off her knee and does her thumb and the first two fingers, which don't come clean.

"Two," Reqa says.

"Two what?" I say.

"Millimetres," Reqa says. "Off the trigger. I took it out of the travel Wednesday and I didn't tell you because I wanted to see what you did with it before you knew."

"What did I do with it?" I say.

"Nothing," Reqa says. "You went out on Friday and you didn't say anything and you came back on Friday and you didn't say anything, and tonight you got seven."

She puts the rag down over the open end of the housing.

"Can I put two more out of it?" Reqa says.

"Is it better?" I ask.

"It's two millimetres," Reqa says. "It isn't better. It's just where your hand is now."

"Take it out," I say.

"I'll do it Sunday," Reqa says.
